## The problems and their tickets

**Shared parser:** it reads dates month first while the affected tenants write day first.
R-41200 describes 7 April becoming 4 July. App, email and booking-v2 form reports use this
path under the exported model. The desk's flagged date-shift reports are excluded from
this ticket because their descriptions do not establish that parser mechanism.

**Form hint:** booking-v3 displays MM/DD/YYYY while interpreting the entry in the
day-first tenant's convention. R-41230 describes 6 January becoming 1 June. This needs
its own fix: correcting the shared parser does not correct misleading form guidance.
The relevant tenant, form version and channel must be considered together.

**One-day / cross-city date shifts:** the desk flagged reports whose descriptions differ
from a month/day swap. R-41202 says Tuesday 12 May became Monday 11 May; R-41620 says
Tuesday 6 October appeared as Monday. Other reports describe differing local days or an
invite arriving before booking. They get a separate investigation ticket now. A proven
common cause is not required to preserve and investigate this distinct reported problem.
A timezone or calendar-conversion defect remains a hypothesis, not a diagnosis.

<IncidentEvidence />

## Why the old triage decision is insufficient

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) and the
[triage guide](/kb/triage/dedupe) merge on normalized subject alone. Their accepted status
does not establish that the rule remains adequate. The
[incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) calls the merged reports one parser issue with one fix
queued, but its own table separates parser and hint. Even that table files the flagged
reports by tenant instead of reading their symptoms. Its “no ticket of its own” wording
is historical export output, not the status of the linked issue records above.

The original classifier and accepted records remain unchanged so this correction is
traceable. The new `problems.ts` allocation applies the desk notes before the classifier;
its counts are ticket coverage, not a claim that every report's root cause is proved.

## Proposed triage rule

1. Preserve each original report and count each ID once. Reconcile conflicting duplicates
   rather than silently choosing one record.
2. Read the body as well as the subject. Check tenant locale, form version and channel;
   intake labels and browser locale alone do not establish the mechanism.
3. Use the supplied classifier as evidence about the input path. When a report does not
   fit its explanation, track that discrepancy separately instead of merging it into a
   known fix merely because the classifier returns the same label.
4. Open an investigation for a distinct reported problem even while its cause is unknown.
   Record what is observed and what evidence would confirm or reject a suspected cause.
   Split that investigation further if reproduction establishes different defects.
5. Verify the parser and form-hint corrections independently. For date-shift reports,
   obtain original input, stored values, intended date or instant, and the relevant zones
   before diagnosing a conversion. Keep report links when moving or splitting work.

### Try the existing classifier

The explorer below demonstrates the original model, not the new problem allocation.
Its initial example has matching subjects but different parser/hint classifications.
Changing report A from form to app changes that classification. It does not read bodies
or apply the desk notes, so its verdict alone must not authorize a merge. For unknown
tenants, “different causes” means only that a shared cause has not been established.

<DedupeExplorer />

## Receipt and consolidation are different events

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

A rise in merges can reflect consolidation of older reports, not the start of a failure.
The chart groups source date portions into Monday–Sunday weeks; it does not convert them
to UTC. The coverage table uses received dates. Neither a fresh render nor this page's
review date refreshes the underlying export.

## Evidence boundaries and follow-up

The export supplies reported symptoms and a model of the two input paths. It supplies no
production implementation, reproduced failures or effective-dated tenant history.
Historical parser/hint assignments therefore use the exported directory. Reports without
exact input/output dates remain weaker evidence, even when assigned to a ticket.

The separate investigation preserves all flagged bodies. In particular, different local
calendar days in Lisbon and Toronto can represent the same instant correctly. R-41344
also reports that the meeting was held twice; that scheduling complaint needs investigation,
not an assumed timezone diagnosis. The invite-before-booking claim needs its timestamps.
Do not close these reports as a parser fix without evidence explaining their behavior.

Source ownership stays with the support desk. The original reports, tenant directory and
`desk-notes.json` remain under `src/lib/data/`; the original rules remain in `causes.ts`
and `locales.ts`. `problems.ts` and `IncidentEvidence.svelte` compute this page's new
allocation. Linked issue records include coverage and engineering follow-up.

If this proposal is accepted, the desk should link it from the triage guide and incident
page and maintain the investigation as evidence arrives. The coverage table links the
filed GitHub issues. No live queue or fix status is inferred
from the preserved export.

